---
import PageHeader from '~/components/ui/PageHeader.astro';
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { REVOLUT_PATH_TEXT, STATEMENT_CHOICES, STATEMENT_TRANSFER_TIP } from '~/data/statement';
import { ISSUES_URL } from '~/navigation';

const [format, range] = STATEMENT_CHOICES.map(({ value }) => value);

const sections = [
  { id: 'statement', title: 'Get your statement' },
  { id: 'use', title: 'Use the app' },
  { id: 'confirm', title: 'Confirming an investment' },
  { id: 'result', title: 'Read the result' },
  { id: 'missing-price', title: 'When a price is missing' },
  { id: 'spreadsheet', title: 'Your Excel spreadsheet' },
  { id: 'google-sheets', title: 'Using Google Sheets' },
  { id: 'settings', title: 'Settings' },
  { id: 'privacy', title: 'Privacy' },
  { id: 'limitations', title: 'Known limitations' },
  { id: 'problem', title: 'Report a problem' },
];

const metadata = {
  title: 'Guide · Statement Portfolio Tracker',
  description:
    'How to get your Revolut statement, use the app, read the result and keep your Excel spreadsheet up to date.',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 md:px-6 pt-14 md:pt-20 pb-24 md:pb-32">
    <PageHeader title="Guide">More details on how the app works.</PageHeader>

    <div class="mt-12 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] lg:gap-16">
      <nav aria-label="On this page" class="hidden lg:block">
        <ul class="sticky top-24 space-y-1 text-sm border-l border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10">
          {
            sections.map(({ id, title }) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={`#${id}`}
                  data-toc={id}
                  class="-ml-px block border-l border-transparent pl-4 py-1 text-muted hover:text-default hover:border-gray-400 data-[active]:text-primary data-[active]:border-primary"
                >
                  {title}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <article
        class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-3xl prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-headings:scroll-mt-24 prose-a:text-primary prose-table:text-base prose-th:text-left"
      >
        <h2 id="statement">Get your statement</h2>
        <p>In the Revolut app:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Tap <b>{REVOLUT_PATH_TEXT}</b>.</li>
          <li>Choose <b>{format}</b> as the format and <b>{range}</b> as the date range.</li>
          <li>{STATEMENT_TRANSFER_TIP}</li>
        </ol>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            <b>Always choose All time.</b> The app needs your whole history, from your first trade until today, to know what
            you still hold. A shorter range lists only the buys and sells inside it, so the results would be wrong or the
            file would be rejected as incomplete.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2 id="use">Use the app</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Step 1: your Revolut statement.</b> Drag the file onto the window, or click <b>Choose statement file…</b>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Step 2 (optional): your Excel spreadsheet.</b> Choose <b>Don’t use a spreadsheet</b> to only see the results
            in the app, or <b>Update my Excel spreadsheet</b>, then pick your file or let the app create one. It
            remembers your choice.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Step 3.</b> Click the green button. The app looks up prices, which takes a few seconds, then shows your investments.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="confirm">Confirming an investment</h2>
        <p>
          Many funds are traded on several stock exchanges under the same code. When the app can’t tell which one is
          yours, it shows a short list; they are usually the same fund at almost the same price. You can:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Pick one:</b> the one whose name and currency match what you see in Revolut.</li>
          <li>
            <b>Always choose the first option:</b> the app uses the first listing now and whenever it can’t tell, which is
            usually right, since the listing whose past prices best match what you paid comes first.
          </li>
          <li><b>Skip this investment:</b> it is left without a current value.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The app remembers your answer. To change it later, use <a href="#settings">Settings</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="result">Read the result</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Investment</td><td>The fund or share, as its code and name.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Invested</td><td>What you paid for what you still hold, fees included.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>% of invested</td><td>How much of all the money you invested went into this one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Worth now</td><td>What it would be worth at the latest price. Approximate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>% of worth now</td><td>Its share of all your investments, by what they are worth now.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Gain or loss</td><td>Worth now minus invested.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          The last row is the total. Investments you have sold completely are not listed. To rename an investment,
          double-click its name (or select it and press <kbd>F2</kbd>); the name is also used in your spreadsheet.
        </p>

        <h2 id="missing-price">When a price is missing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>“unavailable”</b>: the app could not get a current price. The row is grey and left out of the total, and
            the app never guesses a value for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>A value marked *</b>: Yahoo Finance could not be reached, so the last price found on an earlier day is
            used. Point at it to see the date.
          </li>
          <li><b>A value marked (your value)</b>: one you entered yourself.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A line under the table then offers <b>Try again</b>, which looks the prices up again, and <b
            >Enter its value…</b
          >, where you type what the whole investment is worth now, as the Revolut app shows it. You can also type it
          over “unavailable” in your spreadsheet. The app keeps your value until Yahoo Finance has a price again.
        </p>

        <h2 id="spreadsheet">Your Excel spreadsheet</h2>
        <p>
          Each time you press the green button, the app writes two sheets of your file, creating them the first time.
          Close the file in Excel first: the app can’t save a file that Excel has open.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Current Holdings</b>: one row per investment you hold, and a Total. Percentages and the Total are Excel
            formulas, so they follow if you change a value. The whole sheet is rewritten each time, so keep your notes
            elsewhere.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Portfolio History</b>: one row per day you use the app (a second run on the same day updates that row),
            with what you put in, what it is worth, unrealized and realized gains, ROI %, net worth, net deposits and
            gain vs deposits.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <b>Your own sheets:</b> values, formulas, formatting, colour rules, tables and charts are kept, and can read from
          the app’s sheets. <b>Pictures, shapes and text boxes are lost</b>, because the app saves the whole file again;
          if your file has any, create a new spreadsheet instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Backups:</b> the first time each day the app changes an existing file, it saves a copy next to it, named with
          the date, for example <code>My investments_backup_2026-10-15.xlsx</code>. Three are kept by default; you can
          change that in Settings. To go back, open a copy and save it under your spreadsheet’s name.
        </p>

        <h2 id="google-sheets">Using Google Sheets</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Install <a href="https://www.google.com/drive/download/">Google Drive for desktop</a> and sign in. Your Drive
            appears on your computer like a folder.
          </li>
          <li>In step 2, create the spreadsheet inside your Drive, or choose an Excel file already there.</li>
          <li>
            Open it from drive.google.com. Google Sheets edits it as an Excel file (it shows a small .XLSX label).
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          After each update, reload the page to see the new values. Close the file in Google Sheets before pressing the
          green button. <b>File › Save as Google Sheets</b> makes a separate copy, which the app does not update.
        </p>

        <h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
        <p>Settings lists the investments the app remembers. Select one to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Rename</b> it, for the table and the spreadsheet.</li>
          <li>
            <b>Choose the listing next time</b>: the next run shows you the list of listings for it again.
          </li>
          <li><b>Forget</b> it: the next run looks for its listing again, as on the first day.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You can also set how many backup copies to keep (0 to 99), and undo changes to investments with
          <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          Everything happens on your computer. There are no accounts, and the app doesn’t record how you use it. The
          only thing that leaves your computer is the codes of your investments and dates, sent to Yahoo Finance to look
          up prices. Your statement and your amounts are never sent anywhere, and none of the amounts in your statement
          are kept by the app.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app keeps a few kilobytes of settings (your confirmed investments, where your spreadsheet is, last prices
          found and values you entered) in your user folder. Uninstalling it on Windows deletes them.
        </p>

        <h2 id="limitations">Known limitations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Only Revolut’s stock statement in CSV format, with the “All time” range, is supported.</li>
          <li>The statement must be in a single currency.</li>
          <li>
            Only cash top-ups, market buys, market sells and dividends are understood. Anything else (a stock split, a
            withdrawal) stops the app with a message naming the row, so it never shows numbers built on a guess.
          </li>
          <li>Prices need an internet connection.</li>
          <li>The figures are simple: what you paid against what it is worth now. No tax calculations.</li>
          <li>
            English only. The app doesn’t update itself: new versions are on the <a href="/download">download page</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="problem">Report a problem</h2>
        <p>
          Open an issue on <a href={ISSUES_URL}>GitHub</a>. If the app showed an error box, click <b>Copy details</b> there
          and paste the text into the issue. <b>Don’t attach your real statement</b>: it contains your trades and
          amounts. Describe the problem and the row the message mentions instead.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  // Highlight the section being read in the contents.
  const links = new Map(
    Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-toc]')).map((a) => [a.dataset.toc!, a])
  );
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      entries.forEach((entry) => {
        if (!entry.isIntersecting) return;
        links.forEach((a) => a.removeAttribute('data-active'));
        links.get(entry.target.id)?.setAttribute('data-active', '');
      });
    },
    { rootMargin: '0px 0px -70% 0px' }
  );
  links.forEach((_, id) => {
    const heading = document.getElementById(id);
    if (heading) observer.observe(heading);
  });
</script>
